## 11. Platform Services

### 11.1 OpenShift Update Service (OSUS / Cincinnati)

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Hosts the **update graph** locally so a disconnected cluster's Cluster Version Operator can see available upgrade paths and recommended/conditional edges exactly as a connected cluster does via `api.openshift.com`. Without it you must use `--to-image` with digests and lose edge validation. |
| **Package** | `cincinnati-operator` |
| **Namespace / scope** | `openshift-update-service` / single namespace |
| **Channel** | `v1` |
| **Primary CR** | `UpdateService` |

```yaml
# Trust the mirror registry CA for the graph builder
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata: {name: trusted-ca, namespace: openshift-config}
data:
  updateservice-registry: |
    -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
    <mirror registry root CA>
    -----END CERTIFICATE-----
---
# oc patch image.config.openshift.io/cluster --type merge -p '{"spec":{"additionalTrustedCA":{"name":"trusted-ca"}}}'
apiVersion: updateservice.operator.openshift.io/v1
kind: UpdateService
metadata: {name: update-service, namespace: openshift-update-service}
spec:
  replicas: 2
  releases: registry.example.com:8443/openshift/release-images     # from oc-mirror IDMS
  graphDataImage: registry.example.com:8443/openshift/graph-image:latest   # built by oc-mirror when graph: true
```
```bash
POLICY_ENGINE_GRAPH_URI="$(oc get updateservice update-service -n openshift-update-service -o jsonpath='{.status.policyEngineURI}/api/upgrades_info/v1/graph')"
oc patch clusterversion version --type merge -p "{\"spec\":{\"upstream\":\"${POLICY_ENGINE_GRAPH_URI}\",\"channel\":\"eus-4.22\"}}"
oc adm upgrade
```
Production notes: re-run oc-mirror with the new `maxVersion` before each upgrade; the graph-data image must be rebuilt/re-pushed too (oc-mirror does this when `graph: true`).

### 11.2 Cluster Resource Override Operator

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Mutating admission webhook that **overrides container resource requests/limits** per namespace: e.g., force CPU requests to 25% of limits so dev/test namespaces overcommit predictably, or enforce a limit-to-request ratio. Prevents developers from requesting far more than they use and lets you pack non-production workloads densely. Does not replace LimitRange/ResourceQuota — it complements them. |
| **Package** | `clusterresourceoverride-operator` |
| **Namespace / scope** | `clusterresourceoverride-operator` / single namespace |
| **Channel** | `stable` |
| **Primary CR** | `ClusterResourceOverride` (singleton `cluster`) |

```yaml
apiVersion: operator.autoscaling.openshift.io/v1
kind: ClusterResourceOverride
metadata: {name: cluster}
spec:
  podResourceOverride:
    spec:
      memoryRequestToLimitPercent: 50      # request = 50% of memory limit
      cpuRequestToLimitPercent: 25         # request = 25% of CPU limit
      limitCPUToMemoryPercent: 200         # 200% → 2 cores per 1 GiB memory limit
```
Enable per namespace: `oc label ns dev-team-a clusterresourceoverrides.admission.autoscaling.openshift.io/enabled=true`.
Production notes: apply only to non-production namespaces; the override runs after LimitRange defaults are applied, so set LimitRange defaults first.

### 11.3 Node Tuning Operator (built-in) & Performance Profile

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | NTO ships with OCP and manages **TuneD** profiles on all nodes. The **Performance Profile** API (formerly the separate Performance Addon Operator, now merged into NTO since 4.11) configures a node pool for low-latency: reserved vs isolated CPUs, huge pages, real-time kernel, topology manager, NUMA alignment, and IRQ affinity. Required for telco (DU/CU), HFT, HPC, and latency-sensitive VMs. |
| **Package** | none — built in (`openshift-cluster-node-tuning-operator`) |
| **Primary CRs** | `Tuned`, `PerformanceProfile` |

```yaml
# Custom TuneD profile for database workers
apiVersion: tuned.openshift.io/v1
kind: Tuned
metadata: {name: db-workers, namespace: openshift-cluster-node-tuning-operator}
spec:
  profile:
    - name: db-workers
      data: |
        [main]
        summary=DB tuning
        include=openshift-node
        [sysctl]
        vm.swappiness=1
        vm.dirty_ratio=15
        net.core.somaxconn=4096
        [vm]
        transparent_hugepages=never
  recommend:
    - match: [{label: node-role.kubernetes.io/db}]
      priority: 20
      profile: db-workers
---
# Low-latency node pool
apiVersion: performance.openshift.io/v2
kind: PerformanceProfile
metadata: {name: low-latency}
spec:
  cpu:
    reserved: "0-3,32-35"                 # housekeeping (kubelet, OVS, systemd)
    isolated: "4-31,36-63"                # workloads
  hugepages:
    defaultHugepagesSize: 1G
    pages: [{size: 1G, count: 32, node: 0}]
  realTimeKernel: {enabled: false}        # true only with RT subscription + validated apps
  numa: {topologyPolicy: single-numa-node}
  nodeSelector: {node-role.kubernetes.io/worker-ll: ""}
  machineConfigPoolSelector: {pools.operator.machineconfiguration.openshift.io/worker-ll: ""}
  globallyDisableIrqLoadBalancing: true
  workloadHints: {highPowerConsumption: true, realTime: true, perPodPowerManagement: false}
```
Create the matching `MachineConfigPool` `worker-ll` first; applying the profile reboots those nodes. Pods opt in with `Guaranteed` QoS (requests == limits, integer CPUs) and annotation `cpu-load-balancing.crio.io: "disable"`.
Production notes: generate CPU layout with `podman run ... performance-profile-creator` against a `must-gather`; never apply a PerformanceProfile to the control plane or ODF nodes.

